WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.261
What you are building, and why it comes first.

2
00:00:02.541 --> 00:00:15.107
An eval harness is the code that runs your system over a fixed set of inputs, grades every output, aggregates the grades with honest uncertainty, and records the result so you can compare it with the last run.

3
00:00:15.387 --> 00:00:25.819
Once it exists, every change you consider (a new chunker, a reranker, a prompt edit, a cheaper model) becomes an experiment with a number attached, rather than an argument.

4
00:00:26.099 --> 00:00:37.448
Eugene Yan calls the habit eval-driven development: like test-driven development, you define what success looks like before you build, measure a baseline, and check every change against it.

5
00:00:37.728 --> 00:00:51.552
Anthropic’s agent-evals guide recommends the same and adds a practical starting point: 20 to 50 tasks drawn from real failures is enough to begin, because early improvements tend to be large enough to see with a small set.

6
00:00:51.832 --> 00:00:54.051
You grow the set as you learn what fails.

7
00:00:54.331 --> 00:01:02.203
The Studio 3 project asks you to build this harness for your Studio 2 RAG system and use it to make at least two measured improvements.

8
00:01:02.483 --> 00:01:14.216
This lesson sets up the design without solving it for you: the dataset, the graders, judge calibration, the statistics, the CI gate, the experiment log, and the write-up for a stakeholder.

9
00:01:14.496 --> 00:01:18.357
Every part reuses something from weeks 1 to 3 or from the lab.
